; A nested `(or …)` in a `:match` premise.
;
; An `(or …)` is a whole `:match`: the compiler lowers a top-level one to a
; plan per disjunct. A nested one, lowered to an empty plan, would be
; silently wrong in BOTH polarities — this rule would fire with neither p nor
; q in the KB (unsound), and its mirror `(absent (or (p ?x) (q ?x)))` would
; never fire with neither present (incomplete, and permanently: the empty
; sub-plan makes the guard fail, and a failing monotone guard retires its
; candidate).
;
; The loader refuses it, one line on every command, the views and `kb save`
; among them, before any rule is compiled: lift the `(or …)` to the top of
; the `:match`, or split the rule. The regressions `nested-or-*` hold the
; shape refused where only the compiler met it.
;
(relation a Thing :why "a holds of {?1}")
(relation p Thing :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q Thing :why "q holds of {?1}")

(rule nested-disjunction ()
  :match  (and (a ?x) (or (p ?x) (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "a holds of {?x} and so does p or q, so p holds of {?x}")
